certainty is correct?","explanation":"The spread of repeated readings provides an estimate of random uncertainty.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Measurements, Units and Dimensions","fingerprint":"238471f4dae53dc05d37fc00cb90f5a768919b1dd1c53ea62521f6597066cc4a","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Session 1","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Session 1","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"09127682-897b-5d45-b201-940d86e7bef1","text":"A measuring instrument always gives the true value","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"b2b8eff8-4767-5f12-9b53-1cc011db1898","text":"Random errors are removed by changing units","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"0c81313d-1d18-50b2-a6fa-6fd9c5ce1d92","text":"Every uncertainty is caused by carelessness","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"0911e935-ac07-56aa-8a6a-c0518ebde0cd","text":"Repeated readings can be used to estimate random uncertainty","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"e88d4bf8-a37a-5428-9946-fe8ed3459259","position":10,"prompt":"A car moves at 72 km h⁻¹. What is this speed in m s⁻¹?","explanation":"Multiply by 1000/3600: 72 km/h = 20 m/s.","questionKind":"calculation","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Measurements, Units and Dimensions","fingerprint":"975d1da5ae917fcc317fddc46ff50b7c2847ecf528e5cfdf1360f3eacff95dfa","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Session 1","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Session 1","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"ec11101a-cb94-587d-acc7-5f6b5c38bcc2","text":"25.9 m s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"5f7b70ed-97e1-55cc-ad7f-5f00796e9e6b","text":"259.2 m s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"53d6