14198-9c2f-525d-9255-071bd9a42107","position":60,"prompt":"Newton's first law is also called the law of what?","explanation":"The first law describes a body's resistance to changes in its state of motion.","questionKind":"recall","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"5428a05b12247cdd334122db73a48f32ef04e582cc18c31e270bcbae1d3375e9","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"c895f06a-0953-508a-be08-02d6a948184a","text":"inertia","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"4f9f4e40-ad44-5bb1-a8f1-7ee8279d4e92","text":"gravitation","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"16572f73-c03c-567e-8f10-b9b951fce081","text":"moments","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"ac72964f-0dba-5d1f-93bf-382fc9db3c76","text":"conservation of energy","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"c79f5ce6-3334-59d0-8cfc-a696b2a69d74","position":61,"prompt":"According to Newton's second law, the acceleration of a body is directly proportional to what?","explanation":"For constant mass, a = Fnet/m, so acceleration is directly proportional to net force.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"0ef0e9e0307b77614898d8922186b414c93c0c875e83176cb50711238de98748","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"77f83dbe-0cf8-5f14-97b1-ca1914eb9ca7","text":"its mass","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"f5e5555d-e85c-51ee-a46b-a2d95d23cf16","text":"the elapsed time","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"e796295a-8f4d-55bf-8503-fb378409f9b8","text":"its volume","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"a68b6303-7dd7-5456-b93c-837b1049e8ea","text":"the net force","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"d960dd23-07b7-549f-a109-f26e321ae005","position":62,"prompt":"Which pair illustrates Newton's third law?","explanation":"Third-law forces are equal, opposite and act on different bodies.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"93764f6db6cc3964f5af69e145742286cb0e2d318eb32c1aa1fdd99477181632","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"5c2a5250-ab70-5b41-9687-8072428c000a","text":"A stationary book has zero resultant force","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"8d35e581-efc5-50bb-8d71-b63181e56408","text":"A car slows because of friction","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"ab2a6260-b2f8-55a4-8d58-b76b7055e72c","text":"A swimmer pushes water backward and the water pushes the swimmer forward","isCorrect":true,"position":2},{"id":"e34599f9-1f76-58b3-bd34-16cf48040578","text":"A stone falls and becomes faster","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"c535c679-564b-5eba-9abf-83ebcd040748","position":63,"prompt":"Why do action and reaction forces not cancel each other?","explanation":"Equal and opposite third-law forces belong to different free-body diagrams.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"71ec00e1201a01327f472dd9fe7e4c586e8eccd01b085c9d06c7a71b85dd4f06","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"22bda9eb-fefc-556c-b989-0a384cadfdf1","text":"They point in the same direction","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"3c31633d-7945-550d-9ecb-72efcfa76307","text":"They act on different bodies","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"5390cd11-cec4-5246-aa2d-080cee06de47","text":"They act at different times","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"a64d6fb4-cedd-50b9-99ee-ca4178efbd52","text":"One force is always larger","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"3f101c03-945f-5fe2-b0bf-bad2ba38101d","position":64,"prompt":"A body is in translational equilibrium when which condition holds?","explanation":"Zero net force gives zero acceleration; the body may be at rest or move uniformly.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"885fa92bd5b3fa68b18f7856aed432587872f668e4d7d08b8c0079c096201ec0","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"9b4296ea-9f3a-55ce-8cff-44716327974f","text":"The vector sum of external forces is zero","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"b48b1fe8-8356-58c8-a86a-389a3d2352d2","text":"Its speed must be zero","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"4b8db918-4a33-5e9f-8281-427ad48d74f5","text":"Its kinetic energy is maximum","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"16b5b99e-3cbd-533d-bbe1-9b2a62c3cc1f","text":"No individual force acts on it","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"2e8841fe-6eea-5ace-9065-e6ca60172c1e","position":65,"prompt":"What is the direction of the normal reaction on an object resting on a surface?","explanation":"The normal reaction acts normal, or perpendicular, to the contact surface.","questionKind":"recall","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"b56b94307735dcca6203ee91f2c932d1ee0cb3f6d58b44acc3ec93bd9dab0dcd","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"15a336c1-cd78-5869-a6ca-ca7f6a2ff243","text":"Parallel to the surface","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"7074fea4-497d-5fac-8e87-6f219c5f6c98","text":"Always vertically downward","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"d4b6edc9-e3c0-53fa-82ac-ccc0c0904eeb","text":"Opposite to the object's velocity","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"cfdc5ad4-887e-5c50-958b-cee9bee1d9bc","text":"Perpendicular to the surface","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"4a70b4a7-6af9-5fa4-a863-b55339cdd16d","position":66,"prompt":"Static friction on an object generally acts in which direction?","explanation":"Static friction opposes the tendency of surfaces to slide relative to one another.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"52da2eae4f95e66bbd6f3cbfa6e4cfdb22866bb8b843ee45f2a4d59b42dda49a","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"b941f0d7-873c-529d-8396-2658d5ed2711","text":"Perpendicular to the contact surface","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"55d3bafe-ec94-5e74-92e6-02ad3ea927ca","text":"Toward the object's centre of mass","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"7e467330-0e59-524f-8d3c-9a7986ab984b","text":"Opposite the impending relative motion","isCorrect":true,"position":2},{"id":"4da6f833-376b-59f4-8970-95ca0e2b0e1a","text":"Always in the direction of weight","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"58ad02bf-3f96-55e2-a351-b6022815384a","position":67,"prompt":"A passenger lurches forward when a moving bus stops suddenly. This is mainly due to what?","explanation":"The passenger's body tends to continue its forward motion when the bus decelerates.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"ffcda1cbcb379520a1f751eac6d0713dc942503f2ac4947c80feb1235a587e2f","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"2cee6089-0cd9-550c-a592-f9d3905a5c1e","text":"elasticity","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"fe5e462a-59ab-5220-b9d7-98541ce26667","text":"inertia","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"8c8fa41f-7ed8-533f-b098-e4e1cc1302a2","text":"buoyancy","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"19deea9c-3063-5701-9eff-ed7038de53ef","text":"centripetal force","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"319eb10f-0a76-50a2-8779-e77ee0d45686","position":68,"prompt":"When the net force on a moving object is zero, what happens to its velocity?","explanation":"Zero net force means zero acceleration, so velocity is unchanged.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"8a17cc500d85544013eac71a0e5076e88f3edabd7d36212d0e6625c90b30874c","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"d9ca37ba-3be3-5839-adc9-542dc3bae059","text":"It remains constant","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"9631a473-85db-5dbf-85b3-738b5bfab7f7","text":"It immediately becomes zero","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"3b8d577d-91d2-512b-b884-9f4542b64d68","text":"It continually increases","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"6e69a5a6-5311-59c4-9761-47b95cf67d08","text":"It reverses direction","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"535c57dd-a574-5822-a4e5-27e4ee6b608a","position":69,"prompt":"Which force is the reaction partner to Earth's gravitational pull on a falling stone?","explanation":"The Earth pulls the stone and the stone pulls the Earth with equal and opposite forces.","questionKind":"analysis","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"Newton's Laws, Forces and Equilibrium","fingerprint":"4f8f48d700048b2afcbb928847d9da6b121ab6aaf7f4b67b8f8ddc58a65fc648","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Study Sessions 4 and 10","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"a3e48734-f562-5efd-bdfa-cd9c8f825b03","text":"Air resistance on the stone","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"7d570c35-9ee5-5b9e-93fe-0af9b9af6797","text":"The stone's weight and its acceleration","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"63241c40-c000-56a6-959c-94fe24cd6161","text":"The normal force from the ground","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"5c401050-6bbd-5378-8b47-9eafe675cd68","text":"The stone's gravitational pull on Earth","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"65ce4afd-0dbb-56be-856d-f505519603cf","position":70,"prompt":"A net force of 18 N acts on a 6 kg body. What is its acceleration?","explanation":"a = F/m = 18/6 = 3 m/s².","questionKind":"calculation","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"