quantity?","explanation":"Force produces linear acceleration while torque produces angular acceleration.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"021fa737dc16ffae382aa80616a793e8604fa7275c5640a9ad5184c7bd0b2fc8","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"58b0b378-dc7b-5eb9-82f4-7d24c37f0629","text":"force","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"aff1ca11-6285-5d82-addc-59834260f500","text":"mass","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"35669c23-9ff5-5df2-8210-d106a8f0fa40","text":"velocity","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"f64ae16f-a399-562c-909a-6e5b52a55d63","text":"power","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"cd56a731-396d-5198-8274-a090a3c9d743","position":161,"prompt":"Moment of inertia measures a body's resistance to changes in what?","explanation":"Moment of inertia plays the rotational role analogous to mass in translation.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"ff3ef294ba1343cebe2c2fd0f3ab5dd74c031be0f4d4111226b270299670fbe8","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"7dc93816-1da1-5607-aee5-976b0b6c00ab","text":"temperature","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"d0524443-3d69-5de6-af81-4ae375695ddd","text":"linear position only","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"9fe88fa1-e02b-5dc7-a59d-c610b309e209","text":"electric charge","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"8589cd33-2874-5ca0-b2fc-21db6f60a3d3","text":"rotational motion","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"daf1c64d-9f10-52e9-a410-284b273fd652","position":162,"prompt":"Which equation is the rotational analogue of F = ma?","explanation":"Net torque equals moment of inertia times angular acceleration.","questionKind":"recall","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"7b1a404aca0bd3bf2c5ce15059336007b98a283c256e95f741df55a867506c69","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"e549758d-c685-5365-a706-e73e52d23979","text":"τ = mr","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"00e8a7bf-3384-5e76-af38-9e675e1499fa","text":"I = α/t","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"a867ca68-9d48-55e5-b62c-98b566fa3c8d","text":"τ = Iα","isCorrect":true,"position":2},{"id":"292f27d2-b214-56e7-b018-55951c48e416","text":"τ = Iω","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"7c05c112-4472-5c3b-9c11-fc56dcab4064","position":163,"prompt":"Angular momentum of a rigid body rotating about a fixed principal axis is given by what?","explanation":"For fixed-axis rotation, angular momentum is moment of inertia times angular velocity.","questionKind":"recall","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"14a57d20d6fb05966e45faa589cfebcb0921358518ab316d450b01e3083fc05a","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"9dff78ff-80c8-5517-89a6-657f35a0bf52","text":"L = τω","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"8aa63f7b-0a44-5998-8e42-e47978e67c0e","text":"L = Iω","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"8da9f219-a6ec-568e-ae81-ff5ba7c2c50d","text":"L = I/ω","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"7f026e1b-7657-55a0-9c68-195f383a9b95","text":"L = mr","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"bf27a33c-c011-5c08-b9a9-acf7bdc6565f","position":164,"prompt":"When is angular momentum conserved?","explanation":"The rate of change of angular momentum equals net external torque.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"849e9c70c6db2c28d7ef7706032e2db4bc5a579c930d4b4b691a9f831034643c","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"0d4e74ea-3b10-50cf-bc5e-4fff8d95357a","text":"When net external torque is zero","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"b81c1cf7-acee-5013-86a1-e229dc267ada","text":"When angular velocity is zero","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"dd229c39-b803-54ba-b433-2b7049e8b5dc","text":"Whenever kinetic energy decreases","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"018b672b-0c1c-5c3c-ace8-f1d92a4f9725","text":"Only when mass is zero","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"13843262-98c4-5c31-a4bb-a88e06c54c9f","position":165,"prompt":"Why does a spinning skater rotate faster after pulling in their arms?","explanation":"With L = Iω constant, reducing I increases ω.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"635601209beacdc0ea6abd71dc39c281ba9f12ab56ab9e6fb843f7645e16ccb1","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"3fc13b24-47c1-5ee3-bc47-ccc3a997ddfd","text":"Their mass increases","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"478d9bac-e308-5831-9b7e-f5fc23a88de4","text":"External torque becomes infinite","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"a4266356-7af5-50da-a0ae-16c16690c749","text":"Their angular momentum becomes zero","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"10a0a50f-63ac-549d-8ca7-1766805b6c6a","text":"Their moment of inertia decreases while angular momentum is conserved","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"c01f154c-6eac-58c5-90ff-23dda32bf684","position":166,"prompt":"What is the SI unit of torque?","explanation":"Torque is force times perpendicular distance, giving newton metre.","questionKind":"recall","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"3517bb2349485f40e4cf44bab553698fa637fe93e8fb0a2e52091d0055ea5e83","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"5a28936b-ed9e-5485-ac54-bced72f8dd9c","text":"kg m s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"bed3be14-65db-5e3d-84b1-ae6c3db07a55","text":"rad s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"d33a34e5-522a-50ab-9fb7-8d43d21b15c3","text":"N m","isCorrect":true,"position":2},{"id":"fe4ee770-f348-56cb-9073-22ceca149261","text":"N m⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"dec4e3fa-956f-58db-a458-39ad57a8d19a","position":167,"prompt":"For maximum torque from a given force and lever arm, the angle between them should be what?","explanation":"τ = rF sin θ is maximum when sin θ = 1.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"a6732bf35b5a53bb1cbc68c02ce1b045a7ad1a74c9ff15fd479359b2339cf96e","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"0023527f-b6c0-5d39-a3e7-eea4199df88d","text":"180°","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"9758629e-0091-574a-884e-7766c83f0562","text":"90°","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"b10062cb-7ad3-5382-86bb-e71248c48ad6","text":"0°","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"046e79be-5a9a-5987-991c-f7ba10a3b54d","text":"30°","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"4a8eb006-ce9f-5538-b4c6-497ef1535f79","position":168,"prompt":"The rotational kinetic energy of a rigid body is which expression?","explanation":"Rotational kinetic energy is Krot = ½Iω².","questionKind":"recall","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"0839342a913193eb863991fc3b0d5baef474e34d6eb0b0bebacb3c3f09567e2f","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"a0f91d2a-5bfc-5e3e-9e44-775b9b910e63","text":"½Iω²","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"9e9be975-6639-5bc6-b2e1-53f46fe55739","text":"Iω","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"8b61aa08-7258-5a81-b15f-56cbbb6162aa","text":"½mv²","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"bd53532b-df11-571d-bd2e-8b0429803a7e","text":"τω²","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"93892376-ca14-5ed9-8194-1b25ea092fd6","position":169,"prompt":"A body is in rotational equilibrium when which condition holds?","explanation":"Zero net torque gives zero angular acceleration; the body may still rotate uniformly.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"c2f9072931ffe88c476c804bd3e3e972e874e89eccdbd40194606f002065d3cd","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"downloadfile (4), Chapters 1 and 2; PHY 101 NOTE (1), Sessions 12 and 13","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"aba958ef-4307-5235-a965-ad978792bb50","text":"Its angular speed must be zero","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"4ecb2095-c708-5a78-ae7c-81eb787dd697","text":"Its moment of inertia is zero","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"7a904cc7-6bcd-5ec6-85f9-466bbd9a3010","text":"No force acts anywhere","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"2083932c-f5a9-5874-84ba-6b35e1c337ef","text":"The sum of external torques is zero","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"5d38651d-77b7-515f-abb3-c9b958466fed","position":170,"prompt":"A 20 N force acts perpendicular to a lever 0.50 m from its pivot. What torque is produced?","explanation":"τ = rF = 0.50 × 20 = 10 N·m.","questionKind":"calculation","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"e236e