sition":3}]},{"id":"9beb0046-388c-500e-bcdc-68058b896dc0","position":179,"prompt":"A rotating system has I = 6 kg m² and ω = 2 rad s⁻¹. If I decreases to 3 kg m² with no external torque, what is the new angular speed?","explanation":"Conservation I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂ gives 6×2 = 3ω₂, so ω₂ = 4 rad/s.","questionKind":"calculation","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"Rotational Mechanics and Angular Momentum","fingerprint":"fb86b4613213b500fe2472232693ec60421ffd75642a00588ef861c64606cf3b","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"General PHY 101 enrichment: rolling, inertia and angular impulse","sourceKind":"general_phy101","reviewedFromLocalFiles":false},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"General PHY 101 enrichment: rolling, inertia and angular impulse","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"4e4eddeb-b16b-55e3-a291-d35f6985219b","text":"12 rad s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"4c7e4480-68cd-5c46-be04-c520c2a85f4c","text":"4 rad s⁻¹","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"61013e98-71e1-5aae-8380-d7759d9d96b8","text":"1 rad s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"38241ea9-c210-544c-bf64-7d26f2b2ed80","text":"2 rad s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"aff2c14a-0119-56dc-b662-bb7b11ddc806","position":180,"prompt":"Newton's universal law of gravitation states that force varies inversely with what?","explanation":"F = Gm₁m₂/r².","questionKind":"recall","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Gravitation, Kepler's Laws and Satellites","fingerprint":"7f4ee785db37622d58c724ba3f0510cd61e8118848f4fd509a22557d85ade267","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Studies 11 and 12; downloadfile (4), Chapter 3","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Studies 11 and 12; downloadfile (4), Chapter 3","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"f2010229-887f-52ab-8f33-f57b8940adb9","text":"The square of the separation","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"edebb120-b0a0-58c6-af7d-4898ae0fa102","text":"The separation","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"39a2943e-672c-5489-b3a8-3509b5cbf8c0","text":"The product of the masses","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"0a24f5d2-12fe-5a3e-8bb0-002547f0dd88","text":"The square root of each mass","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"3c3fbd45-0bfc-57cb-afbc-5fd033ffd9f5","position":181,"prompt":"Gravitational force between two masses is always what type of force?","explanation":"Ordinary positive masses attract one another gravitationally.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Gravitation, Kepler's Laws and Satellites","fingerprint":"c32e0ef7ad8c3945d522e3057e87cb56d2e3c026af6da6ddbf387b6bcbba1864","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Studies 11 and 12; downloadfile (4), Chapter 3","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"PHY 101 NOTE (1), Studies 11 and 12; downloadfile (4), Chapter 3","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"ce3583e7-e4c4-5717-b721-287cb2c1f7e2","text":"Repulsive","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"f321d2d1-e5d5-555d-9742-081ce0e5d187","text":"Perpendicular to their separation","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"c40cdab5-c083-5695-8221-2910e49171a6","text":"Zero outside Earth","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"d9bdce4d-60b0-5d22-b64e-958fee754485","text":"Attractive","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"9dd3062c-8680-5c19-aee4-3d7cd502615c","position":182,"prompt":"Kepler's first law states that planetary orbits are what?","explanation":"Kepler's first law describes elliptical orbits with the Sun at one focus.","questionKind":"recall","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Gravitation, Kepler's Laws and Satellites","fingerprint":"2cc19ba82be9589f9669c58b973801925df48b48bf705757b1820642420a0a3e","studyRef":{"source