⁻¹","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"9513a2e9-bf0b-5d26-9906-0391385461d7","text":"About 1.13 km s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"a8d26c93-f117-5d38-8c89-ef3ee331e137","text":"About 8.0 km s⁻¹","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"6acc93a3-8277-51a0-83a0-0eb9c0186262","position":196,"prompt":"A satellite's orbital radius is increased by a factor of four. By what factor does its circular orbital speed change?","explanation":"v ∝ 1/√r; increasing r by 4 reduces v by √4 = 2.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"Gravitation, Kepler's Laws and Satellites","fingerprint":"0f35da23bbaf9f2533fe2a01997e0f2690bf51ffb11bc3f8265afb7a9c354f35","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"General PHY 101 enrichment: orbital energy and scaling","sourceKind":"general_phy101","reviewedFromLocalFiles":false},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"General PHY 101 enrichment: orbital energy and scaling","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"913fe651-6914-5798-8a23-24be04443277","text":"It becomes one half","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"4b952b59-3297-586a-9083-ca2ae110add0","text":"It doubles","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"32977c86-efb8-54eb-8f9f-8a7567cccadc","text":"It becomes one quarter","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"e918279b-08c4-575e-b2b5-45bd509240b3","text":"It becomes four times","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"3435943d-ecf5-5ab2-a762-b6ec930f886c","position":197,"prompt":"A planet's orbital radius is four times another's around the same star. What is the ratio of their periods?","explanation":"T ∝ r^(3/2), so 4^(3/2) = 8.","questionKind":"calculation","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"Gravitation, Kepler's Laws and Satellites","fingerprint":"322392dffafa9321c3d99dda6cc140ee94c92bfb660d685c20e4b481e06eca14","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"General PHY 101 enrichment: orbital energy and scaling","sourceKind":"general_phy101","reviewedFromLocalFiles":false},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"General PHY 101 enrichment: orbital energy and scaling","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"846799e9-d948-5656-b26d-395c4d04654a","text":"2:1","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"a190cc6a-5051-5e5b-a6f6-14841fe9c37e","text":"4:1","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"6db080fe-b9d1-5b8d-928d-5e0f40be16e3","text":"16:1","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"189da4a0-8625-5680-a0e3-49ee4055e453","text":"8:1","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"1efe9ebe-95d9-5ef6-bb61-a1c6bc235291","position":198,"prompt":"At a height equal to Earth's radius above the surface, gravitational field strength is what fraction of its surface value?","explanation":"The distance from Earth's centre is 2R, so g' = g(R/2R)² = g/4.","questionKind":"calculation","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"Gravitation, Kepler's Laws and Satellites","fingerprint":"acc09787dc170bb2555e5e0ae93218c79f925bf3f5bc4d945204eb10b951d405","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"General PHY 101 enrichment: orbital energy and scaling","sourceKind":"general_phy101","reviewedFromLocalFiles":false},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"General PHY 101 enrichment: orbital energy and scaling","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"514a3a2b-b06a-5352-b64c-46c6b200a586","text":"1/8","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"1f70466d-7905-5260-b0ad-a38e49ec939e","text":"1/16","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"b74ec3a0-d582-589c-b5af-9e9b63ac1338","text":"1/4","isCorrect":true,"position":2},{"id":"d1e85cb5-30f6-5bcc-b249-f9c1edc652e3","text":"1/2","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"95ac3b3a-81b7-57c8-a8a9-6a79414347b5","position":199,"prompt":"A satellite in a circular orbit has gravitational potential energy U. What is its total mechanical energy?","explanation":"For a circular orbit K = −U/2, hence E = K + U = U/2; both U and E are negative.","questionKind":"analysis","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"Gravitation, Kepler's Laws and Satellites","fingerprint":"fbb2ea17f98387e98754e1d6f6cc72f7875150ba4177770d538a38b058475264","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"General PHY 101 enrichment: orbital energy and scaling","sourceKind":"general_phy101","reviewedFromLocalFiles":false},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_phy101_200_v1","sourceReference":"General PHY 101 enrichment: orbital energy and scaling","scientificallyCrossChecked":true},"options":[{"id":"49f6ff10-d9f8-5f6a-b6f7-7b5fae10e3f8","text":"Zero","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"bd26f9af-1d34-555c-bdba-5e765efed2a8","text":"U/2","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"4798a6b2-e742-5071-aed1-a5478b610135","text":"2U","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"705e1475-bc4c-59f1-bd40-a3f7c5ece095","text":"−U","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]}]$phy101_questions$::jsonb)
  LOOP
    INSERT INTO public.study_quiz_questions
      (id, set_id, prompt, explanation, question_type, position, question_kind,
       difficulty_level, cognitive_level, source_topic, question_fingerprint,
       study_ref, generation_meta, ai_generated, published, created_at)
    VALUES
      ((v_question->>'id')::uuid, v_set_id, v_question->>'prompt', v_question->>'explanation',
       'mcq', (v_question->>'position')::integer, v_question->>'questionKind',
       v_question->>'difficulty', v_question->>'cognitive', v_question->>'topic',
       v_question->>'fingerprint', v_question->'studyRef', v_question->'generationMeta',
       false, true, now());

    FOR v_option IN
      SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(v_question->'options')
    LOOP
      INSERT INTO public.study_quiz_options
        (id, question_id, text, is_correct, position, created_at)
      VALUES
        ((v_option->>'id')::uuid, (v_question->>'id')::uuid, v_option->>'text',
         (v_option->>'isCorrect')::boolean, (v_option->>'position')::integer, now());
    END LOOP;
  END LOOP;

  UPDATE public.study_quiz_sets
  SET questions_count = 200,
      exam_question_count = 40,
      time_limit_minutes = 40,
      published = false,
      visibility = 'private',
      updated_at = now()
  WHERE id = v_set_id;
END
$import$;

COMMIT;

-- Expected result: 200 questions, 800 options, 200 correct options, 0 verified until admin review.
SELECT
  s.id AS set_id,
  s.title,
  count(DISTINCT q.id) AS questions,
  count(o.id) AS options,
  count(o.id) FILTER (WHERE o.is_correct) AS correct_options,
  count(DISTINCT q.id) FILTER (WHERE q.exam_verified_at IS NOT NULL) AS verified_questions
FROM public.study_quiz_sets s
LEFT JOIN public.study_quiz_questions q ON coalesce(q.set_id, q.quiz_set_id) = s.id
LEFT JOIN public.study_quiz_options o ON o.question_id = q.id
WHERE upper(regexp_replace(coalesce(s.course_code, ''), '[^A-Za-z0-9]', '', 'g')) = 'PHY101'
  AND s.delivery_mode = 'mock_exam'
  AND s.exam_campaign_key = 'supplementary-2026'
GROUP BY s.id, s.title;
